        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_49,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_524,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_588,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_785,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_995 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "47064ba19de3260d209ec3657b8cf1ac"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 7 ;
    neo:text "I was once sitting in a car driven by a yogi. When another car passed by us the yogi asked, \"Is it enough for me to know it as meditation awareness if I knew that a car passed by?\" No. It's not quite there yet. We would all know that a car had passed by whether we are meditating or not. How is meditation-knowing different from regular-knowing? There's seeing, hearing, and thinking and the meditator knows consciously that these things are being experienced. A non-meditator knows that a car has passed by but does not reference it to his own experience. He may instead refer just to the concept of what has happened where the thought has already processed the whole experience. That is why I say awareness is when you are aware of the six sense objects as your direct experience."^^xsd:string .

