        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1423,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1424,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1425,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1426,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_23,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_96 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "2b2df2d3c3363928792301f4ad37254d"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 11 ;
    neo:text "You can sit, walk, and lie down as you meditate in your daily lives. The posture is not important, and is even less so in daily life where you have to go about your business doing things. The timetable, however, is all the time from the moment you wake up in the morning until the moment you fall asleep at night."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1427 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1428,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_15,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_201,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_214,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_531,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_536,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_653,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_803,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_804 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "d7fa0c3fedb13e2e30ee9369496d8637"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 12 ;
    neo:text "You also need to know how to view these objects that are arising. Whether they are sense objects or your experience, you need to view them as nature. Nature means it is impossible to find a \"you\" or a \"me.\" Nature is all just a process of cause and effect. There's nothing inherently good or bad in these objects."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1429 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1063,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_110,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_111,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1430,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_214 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "662a91115c67e7adf3ddb31849da28ad"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 13 ;
    neo:text "If you are developing greed or aversion towards any kind of sound, it indicates that you have a wrong view of the experience."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1431 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1063,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_110,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_111,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1430,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_177,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_201,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_204,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_214,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_256,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_44,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_84,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_994 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "11cef119615847a6c2f019789168c216"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 14 ;
    neo:text "Come back to the right view when you face difficulties with certain objects: consider sound as just a sound. It is an object and it is just nature. This is the view that you should take. The instruction in the Satipatthana Sutta is to recognize the experience as it is. If you are developing greed or aversion towards any kind of sound, it indicates that you have a wrong view of the experience. For someone with right view, samadhi, or stability of mind is automatically there."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1432 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1063,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1433,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1434,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1435,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1436,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_54,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_69,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_785,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_794,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_795 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "27bd1cb69359fbdae3fc4d6287539b57"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 15 ;
    neo:text "A concept is just a name given to reality. When you become aware of reality, you can see both the mind that is conceptualizing and reality. You will notice a sound as just a sound (reality) and also an interpretation of the particular sound (concept). When you are crossing the street and you hear a car coming, then you also need to watch out to see if a car is coming. You can know that sound is just sound but wisdom must also understand what that sound means. There are two kinds of wisdom at work here: the wisdom that understands the concept of an approaching car and the wisdom that understands reality of a sound is a sound."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1437 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1438,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_167,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_177,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_214,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_558,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_795 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "11a268821f2f83134e582f23749e8fb8"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 16 ;
    neo:text "Understanding is different from just experience. Understanding means you get it: This is reality. Sometimes experience does not mean that you truly understand it and the view changes. You can know that something is an object, which you can't touch or feel but you just understand that this is something that is being known and therefore has the role of being an object."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1441 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1026,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_111,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_167,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_18,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_23,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_54,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_588,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_750,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_829 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "4aac7bd1a3c99d3164eccc1086922725"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 17 ;
    neo:text "The same instructions apply for the thinking mind. When you notice that the mind is thinking, know it. If there is less thinking, know that. If there is more thinking, know that. If there is no thinking, know that. Thinking is the mind and the definition of mind is that which thinks and that which knows. Problems arise when there is aversion to thinking, when you have the idea that you should not be thinking when you are meditating. All sorts of resistance follow that idea. There is no need to struggle with your thoughts because the thinking mind is never a problem. If there is a lot of thinking going on, there is no need to reduce it! All you have to do is acknowledge over and over again that the mind is thinking. After all, you are only able to recognize that amount of thinking because there is awareness!"^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1442 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_103,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1100,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1443,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1444,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_210,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_246,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_39,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_442,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_54,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_693 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "1bef48a033aab052f158a037ee722d24"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 18 ;
    neo:text "Yogis label the times when they are lost in thought and don't know that they're thinking because they are lost in thoughts as a wandering mind. The mind arises in the present moment and passes away in the present moment. Thoughts about home or work arise in the mind and we can acknowledge these thoughts arising each time it happens. Actually the mind does not wander anywhere nor does it go anywhere; it thinks about the past and future in the present moment."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1445 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1026,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_111,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1446,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1447,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1448,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1449,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_18,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_201,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_504,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_527,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_54,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_600,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_84 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "6c62d9f3c53c0caa6800d180da21c0b4"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 19 ;
    neo:text "You can relate to pain in the same way. When you notice even a little bit of pain, first check your attitude. If the mind is feeling uncomfortable, adjust the attitude, remind yourself that this is nature and then, having brought in the right view, watch the discomfort or the aversion in the mind. First, watch the resistance and then you can watch the actual physical sensations. To begin with, why do you observe pain? Do you want to make it go away? It is natural for the body to become uncomfortable if we sit still for long periods. We should observe pain because we want to understand something about the nature of pain; we don't observe pain in hopes that it will go away. Why do you think your leg hurts when it hurts? The simplest answer is that your leg hurts because you have a leg. Where there is a body, there will also be physical pain. That is awareness and right view that we can develop."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1450 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1451,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1452,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1453,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1454,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1455,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1456,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_18,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_223,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_23,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_49,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_54,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_68 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "521e7bd3d301a4bf370f1c871cb46d4e"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 20 ;
    neo:text "The final, important piece of a yogi's three jobs is to practice continuously. Whatever you do, first be aware of yourself, and then go about your task. You may have fairly continuous mindfulness when you are sitting in meditation but you will eventually get up. What do you know first when you get up? It could be your hand or legs moving or just the energy you're using to put strength into what you do. Know that and what's happening after that. It's even better when you know all your movements and if you know what is happening in the mind at the same time."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1457 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1043,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1380,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1458,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1459,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_223,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_530,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_531,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_54,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_837 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "53ee0f9c694a192cd5f3ae733c980227"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 21 ;
    neo:text "When you do sitting meditation you can take the whole body as an object and know anything that becomes obvious and see it change. If you are used to using an anchor, then use that anchor as a starting point. You can use awareness of breathing in and out at the nostrils (anapanasati), rising and falling of the abdomen, or sounds as anchors. These are just a few examples. You can use any object that is easy for you to be aware of as an anchor to establish mindfulness. The anchor just starts the mind off on being mindful and once you are aware of any object for some time, the mind will actually start to know other objects. That is good. It means that mindfulness is getting better. When the mindfulness is good, it begins to feel like it knows four or five objects simultaneously."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1460 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1461,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1462,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_15,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_168,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_170,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_527,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_54,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_545 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "5c01967adc67319b9929d1bbcd3f28db"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 22 ;
    neo:text "You are observing everything to understand the nature of this mind- body experience. What can you understand about these experiences? You have been practicing for a long time, you have been watching the body, watching the mind, and you have been doing it for years. What more have you understood about this body and this mind and the interactions between body and mind?"^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1463 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1464,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1465,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1466,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_23,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_240,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_368,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_558,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_616 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "aab2a0fa80d959414f60dcc8f5d3e604"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 23 ;
    neo:text "The goal of Satipatthana practice is to understand deeply. When there is right understanding, when we have true realization into the nature of things, there will be calm in the mind. This calm state is a by-product of understanding or insight. The real goal of this meditation is to understand."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1467 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1026,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_110,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_111,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1421,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1468,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_504,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_54 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "c306799714e5e83fab8668908a1c474a"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 24 ;
    neo:text "How can we understand the nature of pain if we just stared at it and it disappeared? Please be patient. When there is pain, watch the resistance in the mind and do this only for as long as it is bearable. When you change your posture, be aware of two possible motivations behind it: 1) greed to be more comfortable, or 2) understanding that if you continue to sit in that position, your aversion will grow. You don't want to be growing aversion any more than you want to be growing greed. Understanding that the pain might become too much, you let go of the pain and make yourself more comfortable to prevent the growth of aversion."^^xsd:string .

ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1469 a neo:Document ;
    neo:MENTIONS ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_1470,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_195,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_223,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_481,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_54,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_584,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_588,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_96 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Appendix: Mindfulness in Brief"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "d5b24a2130b3361b2db3ccba58dc2a8b"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 25 ;
    neo:text "Finally, let's discuss talking. We should try to practice right speech and only say what is necessary and wholesome and for this we do need mindfulness. Speaking is all around us in daily life. You do not want to cut off speech without awareness of what you need to do. Use some restraint and know, consider and understand before you speak. Then you will also know if there is a need to talk. If you wanted to say something and you refrained from speaking then continue to know and recognize that you are not speaking but see the thinking going on in the mind."^^xsd:string .

