        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_637,
        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_69 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Mindfulness is a Lifestyle Change"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h3 "GETTING IN THE RING"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "ced6a799b2c33ef86bc1f4fc08a084fa"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 2 ;
    neo:text "When I practiced at home, I would talk with my teacher once every week or so. If you are practicing at home and do not have anyone to ask, ask yourself the question then set it aside. Later, consider what I've said and what the Buddha taught. What have you tried before? What has worked? Should you try one way or another way? Even those who do not meditate will sit down and really think through a life problem if it matters enough to them. The thinking we are referring to with meditation is not just random, abstract thinking but right thinking and use of wisdom."^^xsd:string .

