    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Take a Closer Look"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h3 "GOING THROUGH ROADBLOCKS"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "7a52cb71fd8dd7ed2c079cf2e1593f74"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 1 ;
    neo:text "It's natural to have challenges arise in daily life and you may respond to these obstacles in different ways. You may solve the problem in the moment to make yourself feel better temporarily, but that brief relief won't uncover the Second Noble Truth or the cause of suffering. Remember that there is no flyover or underpass on the Noble Path; you have to go through town with whatever experience that arises. The lessons are all laid out for you, and you have to recognize it as a lesson, a chance to work with the obstacle and come to a realization that can free you. The real benefit of the Dhamma is in having looked at defilements and having understood them, to approach the same tasks with wisdom. We do not stop engaging with life issues. When the mind truly comes to understand something that is wrong as wrong, it will not repeat the same mistake again. Understanding will then mature and you can handle life issues wisely."^^xsd:string .

