        ex:node_4_2970e5b0_c019_4056_86a0_f3b0ab208a9b_851 ;
    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Reflect. Learn. Keep Going."^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h3 "HOW TO WORK WITH DIFFICULTIES"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "75ae72457b59a1df063446a158024b82"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 7 ;
    neo:text "If any of the Brahma Viharas, or the \"sublime states\" of loving kindness, compassion, sympathetic joy, and equanimity arise, the mind will feel good and we will feel at peace and ease in understanding of the way things are. If there is compassion without wisdom, that compassion might actually be contaminated because it is mixed up with a non- acceptance of what actually is; a feeling of helplessness will be in there. If that happens, you will need to clear the mind first. Come back to observing the unwholesome mind state with right view. Do not try to force yourself to have equanimity. Work with the unwholesome states that arise until the mind comes to some balance. Then you will recognize whether it is any of the Brahma Viharas arising in the mind."^^xsd:string .

