    neo:h1 "COLLECTING GOLD DUST: Nurturing the Dhamma in Daily Living"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:h2 "Dear Reader"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:id "15051f88f2ddd471fa2d6d3e1ab6191a"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_name "2019_-_Sayadaw-U-Tejaniya-Collecting-Gold-Dust-Web-Book-1_-_local_converter.md"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:jejune_source_slug "collecting-gold-dust"^^xsd:string ;
    neo:paragraph_number 6 ;
    neo:text "This book was compiled as a continuing thread to the question of what net? We each have our own versions of the first few days of retreat ecitement calming down to a steady mind, building momentum over a week or two, only to see it crumble in the anticipation of reentering daily life. Forgetfulness sets in as we leave Sayadaw's presence and the supportive environment of the center. Even in the occasional outing to the village or city while at the Shwe Oo Min Meditation Center, yogis often report to Sayadaw that there are just so many objects out there! To which Sayadaw often replies that there are only six sense doors and knowing is of only the six senses at the six sense doors inside and out of retreat. Sayadaw noted in a recent retreat in California that most of us meet a limited set of people in our daily lives and go about our lives with a limited set of people, pointing out that if we observed our reactions to others with right view, we could learn to resolve these challenges ourselves. Mostly we don't watch and often not with right view."^^xsd:string .

